export const storyArticles = {

  "SR-2026-0003": {
    readingTime: "7 min read",
    metaDescription: "The first period is where sleep, travel, school timings and classroom judgement meet. An Indian study of adolescents shows why the morning can arrive before the learner is ready for it.",
    hero: imageBlock(images.hero, "The first period begins before the teacher has taught a word."),
    blocks: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "At 7:55 on a school morning, a classroom can be full without being fully awake. Bags are under desks. Water bottles appear on tables. Someone is still finishing a conversation from the corridor. A teacher has written the first instruction on the board, but the room has not quite caught up with it." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This is easy to mistake for a classroom-management problem. Sometimes it is. Sometimes it is simply the visible end of a much longer morning." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The morning starts before the school day does" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "For an adolescent, the first period is the last point in a chain that began hours earlier. There is a wake-up time, a commute, breakfast or the absence of it, uniforms, traffic, assembly, a bus stop, a shared auto, a crowded gate. By the time a student opens a notebook, the school has inherited whatever happened before the bell." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The timing of that bell matters. A 2023 study by researchers at AIIMS Bhopal compared adolescents in two schools with different start times: 7 a.m. and 8 a.m. The study included 640 students aged 11 to 18. The students at the later-starting school reported longer sleep; 72.9% were classified as sleep-deprived compared with 87.1% at the earlier-starting school. The earlier-starting group also reported more daytime sleepiness and poorer sleep quality." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The numbers do not tell us what happens in every Indian classroom. They do something more modest and more useful: they make it harder to pretend that the state of a first-period class is produced entirely inside the classroom." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "A tired room does not always look tired" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The noisy student is the easy one to notice. The head on the hand. The repeated request to go to the washroom. The conversation that keeps restarting." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The quieter signs are harder. A student copies the example but does not attempt the next question. Another looks directly at the board and contributes nothing. A whole class follows instructions with unusual obedience and very little curiosity. The room is orderly. The learning is harder to read." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This is where the teacher's first judgement is often less dramatic than the phrase 'engagement strategy' makes it sound. Do I wait? Do I repeat the instruction? Do I ask for an answer from everyone before taking one aloud? Do I keep the planned pace? Do I change the task?" },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The useful response is often smaller than the problem" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A teacher cannot repair a student's sleep in a fifty-minute lesson. Nor can a starter activity compensate for an early school start, a long commute or a difficult morning at home." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "What the teacher can do is reduce unnecessary friction. One instruction instead of four. An example before independent work. A few extra seconds before the first answer. A retrieval question that reconnects the class with yesterday rather than demanding that everyone arrive intellectually at full speed." },
      { type: "quote", text: "The first period is not a blank page. It is where the school day arrives.", attribution: "Staffroom Review" },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "India has been studying the classroom at a finer resolution" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "There is a useful parallel in ASER's classroom-observation work. In its 2024 rural report, a 'deep dive' observed 45 lessons across 24 Std II classrooms in 24 schools spread across eight states. Observers took classroom snapshots every eight minutes, recording what the teacher was doing, what most students were doing and which materials were being used." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The method is revealing because it resists the temptation to describe a lesson with one label. A classroom changes during a lesson. The teacher changes activity. Students change what they are doing. Materials change. Attention changes. A single judgement at the end can miss the sequence." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The same is true of a tired first period. 'The class was tired' is too blunt to be useful. Which students? At what point? During which task? After which transition? Did the class become more alert when it had something concrete to do? Did the quietest students become more visible when the teacher stopped asking for hands?" },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The timetable is part of the pedagogy" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Teachers are usually asked to adapt to the timetable, not design it. Yet the timetable determines when concentration is expected, when movement is possible and how much recovery sits between demanding periods." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That does not mean every school should simply start later. Schools have transport arrangements, family schedules, staff contracts, local conditions and competing obligations. It does mean that timing belongs in the educational conversation. If the same cohort arrives depleted every morning, the problem deserves to be noticed before it is translated into a story about effort." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "What the teacher can know—and what the teacher cannot" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A teacher can know that a class is slower than usual. A teacher can notice that a particular task is not landing. A teacher can compare one morning with another. A teacher cannot know from a lowered head whether a child slept badly, travelled for two hours, is worried about an exam or is simply bored." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That distinction matters. Observation is useful precisely when it remains observation. The moment it becomes a confident explanation without evidence, it starts doing harm." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "So the first-period skill is not diagnosing tiredness. It is reading the room carefully enough to choose the next move, while leaving room for uncertainty." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The first lesson still has to be taught" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The bell will not wait for perfect conditions. The work still has to begin." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "But beginning well is not the same as beginning loudly, quickly or exactly as planned. A teacher can enter with a route rather than a script. The room can tell the teacher something. The teacher can respond without lowering the intellectual ambition of the lesson." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "By the time the second period arrives, the morning will already look different. Some students will have woken up. Some will not. The teacher will have made a dozen small adjustments that nobody will record." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That is the point. The first period is not a test of whether a teacher can overcome tired children. It is a test of whether a school can notice the conditions under which learning is being asked to happen." },
    ],
    sources: [
      { label: "Singh et al., “Impact of school start time and sleep pattern of adolescents on their mood and sleep quality” — Journal of Family Medicine and Primary Care / AIIMS Bhopal", url: "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10706506/" },
    ],
  },

  "SR-2026-0004": {
    readingTime: "8 min read",
    metaDescription: "Meetings are meant to make school work easier. When coordination becomes the work itself, teachers lose the time that good coordination was supposed to protect.",
    hero: imageBlock(images.school, "A school is held together by decisions that happen beyond the classroom."),
    blocks: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A meeting begins with a familiar promise: this will make the week easier. Someone has brought a list. Someone else has brought a problem. The people around the table are there because they have to agree on something before everyone else can get on with the work." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Then the hour changes shape. A decision that could have taken five minutes opens three new questions. A question that belongs to another meeting arrives early. Someone remembers an issue from last term. A timetable is opened. Another calendar is checked. By the time the meeting ends, the school has made progress, but the people in the room have also lost a piece of the time in which they were supposed to do everything else." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Coordination is real work" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "It is easy to write about meetings as though they are the opposite of work. They are not. Schools need people to coordinate rooms, schedules, support, communication, safeguarding, curriculum decisions and the countless dependencies created when hundreds of people share one institution. A school with no coordination would not be freer. It would simply move its confusion somewhere else." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The problem begins when the meeting becomes the default answer to every kind of uncertainty. If two people need to decide something, invite six. If a document is unclear, call everyone together. If a problem is recurring, schedule a standing meeting. If nobody knows who owns a decision, put it on the agenda." },
      { type: "quote", text: "A meeting can solve a coordination problem while creating a time problem of its own.", attribution: "Staffroom Review" },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The hidden cost is fragmentation" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The obvious cost of a long meeting is the time spent sitting in it. The less visible cost is what happens around it. A teacher leaves a planning task unfinished because a meeting starts at four. A colleague postpones a conversation because there is no ten-minute gap. A decision waits because the next available meeting is a week away. Work is pushed into the edges of the day, where it becomes harder to see and easier to treat as the teacher's own problem." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This is why meeting culture cannot be judged only by the number of hours on a calendar. The more useful question is what kind of working day the calendar creates. Two schools can have the same number of meetings and very different experiences of time if one protects preparation, follow-up and uninterrupted work while the other repeatedly breaks them apart." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "What belongs in a meeting?" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A useful meeting has a job that conversation is genuinely suited to do. It may require a group decision, a difficult judgement, the sharing of information that changes what several people will do next, or a discussion in which the participants need to hear one another before deciding." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Much else can be handled differently. Information can be written down. Routine updates can be circulated. A decision can sometimes be made by the person who owns it, with others informed afterwards. A complicated issue can be prepared in advance so the meeting is used for the point of disagreement rather than for reading the background aloud." },
      { type: "list", items: ["Give every meeting a decision or concrete output.", "Invite only people whose presence changes the decision or the work that follows.", "Send information before the meeting when participants need time to read it.", "End when the work is complete rather than when the calendar slot ends.", "Record the decision, owner and next action so the same question does not return unchanged."] },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The school day should remain visible" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The deepest question is not whether a school has too many meetings. It is whether its coordination system remembers what the meetings are there to protect. The point of organising a school is not to create a beautifully organised calendar. It is to make teaching, learning, care and professional judgement possible." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A meeting culture that protects those things can be demanding and still be humane. It asks people to decide, to communicate clearly and to take responsibility for what happens next. It does not confuse more conversation with better coordination." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The test is simple enough to use after the next meeting. What became clearer? What decision was made? What work became easier? If the answers are difficult to find, the problem may not be that the school needs another meeting. It may be that the meeting has quietly become the work." },
          { type: "paragraph", text: "That test also makes meetings easier to shorten. If the purpose is a decision, put the decision near the beginning. If people need information before they can decide, circulate it first. If only one person can act, do not make the entire staff sit through the handover." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "None of this is an argument for fewer conversations at any cost. Some of the hardest work in a school is conversational because relationships, competing needs and professional disagreement cannot be reduced to a form. The point is to protect the distinction between a conversation that changes the work and a meeting that merely records that the work is difficult." },

    ],
    sources: [],
  },

  "SR-2026-0058": {
    readingTime: "7 min read",
    metaDescription: "A visual inventory of the objects, notes, screens and small routines that make up a teacher's week, and what those objects reveal about the work.",
    hero: imageBlock(images.visual, "The objects of teaching are often ordinary. Their arrangement tells a different story."),
    blocks: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A teacher's week rarely looks like a neat sequence of lessons. It is a collection of objects moved from one surface to another: books waiting to be marked, a notebook with three unfinished lists, a mug that has gone cold, a timetable with something crossed out and rewritten, a message that needs answering before the next class." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Look closely and the week becomes visible item by item." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The timetable" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The timetable is supposed to describe time. In practice it also describes interruption. A blank space may be preparation. A crowded sequence may mean that preparation has been pushed elsewhere. A handwritten change in the margin can tell the story of a day more honestly than the original grid." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The exercise books" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A stack of books is both evidence and promise. It records what students have attempted, what they have misunderstood and what the teacher has not yet had time to respond to. The stack grows quietly. It does not announce itself as urgent until the next lesson depends on what is inside it." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The notebook" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Teacher notebooks often contain several kinds of writing on the same page: a reminder about a student, a phrase for tomorrow's explanation, a list of photocopies, an idea that arrived during lunch and a note that says simply, 'Ask again.' The page is less a record than an external memory." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The red pen" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The pen is the least interesting object until it stops working. Then its role becomes clear. Marking is not just correction. It is a conversation delayed by time, a way of telling a student what to notice when the teacher is not in the room." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The laptop" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The laptop contains the visible and invisible school. Lessons, messages, attendance, documents, calendars and drafts share one screen. It makes work portable, which can be useful and can also make the boundary between school and home harder to see." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The chair beside the desk" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A chair pulled slightly away from a desk can mean a conversation happened there. A second chair can mean someone stayed after the bell. The physical arrangement of a room records small decisions that are rarely written down." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The empty space" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Perhaps the most revealing item is the thing that is not there: the uninterrupted hour that would have allowed everything else to be finished. Teachers often build a working week out of fragments. The objects accumulate because the time required to deal with them has been divided into pieces." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Seen this way, a teacher's week is not a pile of tasks. It is a system of relationships between objects, people and time. A book creates a response. A response creates a conversation. A conversation changes a plan. The changed plan produces another note." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The ordinary objects are not the story because they are special. They are the story because they stay after the headline events have passed. They show what teaching asks a person to remember, carry, decide and begin again." },
    ],
    sources: [],
  },

  "SR-2026-0059": {
    readingTime: "5 min read",
    metaDescription: "Changing a seating plan looks small. In a classroom, where students sit can change attention, conversation and what a teacher notices.",
    hero: imageBlock(images.hero, "A classroom arrangement can shape the work that happens inside it."),
    blocks: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A seating plan can look like an administrative detail. Names in boxes. A few arrows. Perhaps a version printed and pinned near the desk. Then the students arrive and the boxes become a room." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Where someone sits affects what they can see, who they speak to, how quickly they ask for help and how easily a teacher can reach them. It can change the volume of a conversation without anyone deciding to change the conversation." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Small changes become visible quickly" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Move one student closer to the front and the reason may be attention. Move another beside a different classmate and the reason may be collaboration. Separate two students and the change may be about reducing distraction. Put someone near the door and the decision may have nothing to do with behaviour at all." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The same seat can mean different things on different days. A student who needs to see the board may need the front. A student who needs a little space may need the edge. A student who rarely speaks may need a neighbour who makes speaking easier rather than a position that simply makes them more visible." },
      { type: "quote", text: "The seating plan is one of the few parts of a lesson a teacher can change before the lesson begins.", attribution: "Staffroom Review" },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The danger of treating the room as fixed" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Once a seating plan has worked for a while, it can become invisible. That is useful until the needs of the room change. A group settles. A friendship shifts. A new student arrives. A task changes. A student who once needed proximity now needs independence." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The plan should therefore be treated as a working hypothesis, not a permanent map of who belongs where. The useful question is not whether the arrangement looks orderly. It is whether the arrangement is helping the particular work of this class." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Notice before you move" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The best reason to change a seat is usually something concrete that the teacher has noticed. Who is being interrupted? Who is doing more talking than thinking? Who has stopped asking questions? Who is difficult to reach during independent work? Which students seem to change when a particular person is nearby?" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "These questions make the seating plan part of observation rather than punishment. A move can then be explained as a response to learning rather than as a judgement about a child." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "There is also value in changing a room for reasons that have nothing to do with a problem. Pairing students differently can create new routes into discussion. A different layout can make collaboration possible. Even a small change can remind a class that the room is designed for the work they are doing now, not simply inherited from last term." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A seating plan is small because it is easy to change. That is also why it matters. It gives a teacher one more way to say, before the lesson starts, that the room can be arranged around what the students need next." },
    ],
    sources: [],
  },

  "SR-2026-0060": {
    readingTime: "5 min read",
    metaDescription: "Ordinary school days contain the small decisions and routines that rarely become stories but make teaching, learning and care possible.",
    hero: imageBlock(images.staffroom, "Much of school life is made from moments that never become headline events."),
    blocks: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Most school days do not contain a dramatic turning point. There is no breakthrough worth putting on a poster. No perfect lesson. No single conversation that explains the whole week." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "There is instead the ordinary work: a teacher notices a student has gone quiet, a colleague shares a worksheet, someone finds a missing book, a lesson starts two minutes late because the previous conversation needed another minute. A question is answered in the corridor. A child who usually leaves quickly stays behind." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The ordinary is not the same as unimportant" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "School stories often gather around extremes because extremes are easier to describe. The first day. The exam result. The difficult meeting. The exceptional student. The crisis that everyone remembers." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "But institutions are mostly maintained by repetitions. People arrive. Rooms are opened. Work is checked. Instructions are clarified. Someone notices what has been missed. Someone else remembers what happened yesterday." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That repetition can be tiring, but it is also where culture becomes visible. A school says what matters through the things people repeatedly make time to do." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Small acts carry the shape of a school" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Consider a student who cannot find a place to start a task. One teacher may repeat the instruction. Another may sit beside the student and make the first step smaller. Neither response needs to become a philosophy of teaching. It is simply a choice made in a particular minute." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The same is true among adults. A colleague can answer a question without making the person asking feel foolish. A department can leave useful notes for the person teaching the next class. A leader can explain why a decision was made instead of only announcing it." },
      { type: "quote", text: "A school becomes itself through what happens when nobody is making a speech about what a school should be.", attribution: "Staffroom Review" },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "What ordinary days leave behind" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "At the end of a school day, the visible evidence can be modest. Boards are wiped. Chairs move. Books return to shelves. The building becomes quieter. Yet the day has altered hundreds of small expectations: who trusts whom, who knows where to go, who feels able to ask, who believes that an adult will notice." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "None of this means that every small act is equally important or that schools should romanticise busyness. Ordinary work can be inefficient, repetitive or unnecessarily difficult. Paying attention to it is useful precisely because it makes those patterns easier to see." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The ordinary school day deserves attention because it is where the publication's larger questions become practical. What does a school value? How does a teacher decide? What does care look like when there is no ceremony around it?" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The answer is usually somewhere between the bell and the next thing that needs doing." },
    ],
    sources: [],
  },

  "SR-2026-0061": {
    readingTime: "8 min read",
    metaDescription: "“Good teaching” sounds self-evident until a school has to decide what it means. The phrase contains competing ideas about attention, judgement, knowledge and care.",
    hero: imageBlock(images.hero, "Good teaching is easier to name in the abstract than to recognise in a particular room."),
    blocks: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "“Good teaching” is one of those phrases that seems clear until someone asks what it means. The room becomes quieter. People begin to offer examples. A well-planned lesson. Strong subject knowledge. High expectations. Relationships. Questioning. Assessment. Independence. Engagement." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "None of these answers is wrong. None is enough on its own." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The difficulty is that teaching is not a single action performed under stable conditions. It is a sequence of judgements made in response to people, knowledge, time and circumstance. A technique that helps one class can make another less responsive. A lesson that looks untidy can contain more thinking than one that looks perfectly controlled. A quiet room can be attentive, confused or simply tired." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The temptation to turn quality into a checklist" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Schools need shared language. Without it, professional conversations can become vague and difficult to act on. The temptation is therefore to make quality visible through a list: look for this, record that, count the other thing." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Checklists can be useful when they protect essentials. They become less useful when the presence of a feature is treated as proof of quality. A teacher can ask many questions without giving students enough time to think. A lesson can contain a clear objective without making the underlying idea clear. A classroom can look calm while students are doing very little intellectual work." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The checklist problem is not that observation is wrong. It is that observation can become detached from purpose." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Start with what students are trying to learn" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A more useful starting point is the work of the lesson itself. What are students being asked to understand, practise, question or make? What would count as a meaningful change in their understanding?" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Once that is clear, teaching decisions become easier to interpret. A long explanation may be exactly right when an idea is unfamiliar. It may be unhelpful when students need to attempt something. A discussion may deepen thinking in one lesson and become a way of avoiding difficult practice in another." },
      { type: "quote", text: "Good teaching is not a collection of visible moves. It is the quality of the judgement connecting those moves to the work of learning.", attribution: "Staffroom Review" },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Attention is part of the job" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Teachers are often asked to hold several kinds of attention at once. There is the planned attention of the lesson and the unplanned attention of the room: the student who has stopped writing, the pair that has become stuck, the question that reveals a misconception, the expression that says the explanation has moved too quickly." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This is why good teaching cannot be reduced to delivering a plan. The plan matters because it gives the lesson direction. Responsiveness matters because the room provides information the plan could not contain in advance." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Knowledge still matters" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "There is another risk in making teaching entirely about responsiveness: the idea that a good teacher simply follows the room. That is not enough either. Students need access to knowledge they do not already have, and teachers need enough subject understanding to choose examples, sequence ideas, identify errors and recognise when a question matters." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Professional judgement depends on knowledge. It also depends on knowing what the knowledge is for." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Care is not the whole definition" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Relationships matter because students learn with people, not with an abstract curriculum. But kindness alone cannot define good teaching. A teacher can be warm and still set work that teaches very little. High expectations without care can become brittle. Care without intellectual ambition can become another form of low expectation." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The better question is how these things work together. Does the classroom communicate that students are worth teaching carefully? Does the work give them something worth thinking about? Does the teacher notice when the planned route is not working? Does the structure of the lesson make it possible for students to practise, question and improve?" },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "A definition that leaves room for judgement" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Perhaps good teaching is best understood less as a performance and more as a relationship between intention and response. The teacher begins with a purpose, chooses a route, watches what happens and adjusts without losing sight of the destination." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That definition is deliberately less tidy than a checklist. It has to be. Teaching is a professional practice conducted in changing conditions, with incomplete information and real people in the room." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This does not make evaluation impossible. It makes evaluation more demanding. A useful conversation about teaching should be able to ask not only what happened, but why that choice made sense, what the students were being asked to do, what the teacher noticed and what changed as a result." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The phrase “good teaching” will probably remain useful because schools need to talk about quality. The challenge is to keep the phrase open enough to support professional judgement rather than closing it down." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The best definition may therefore be a question: what did the teacher make possible here that would not have happened otherwise?" },
    ],
    sources: [],
  },

  "SR-2026-0065": {
    readingTime: "9 min read",
    metaDescription: "Teaching is a chain of small judgements. Indian school data and classroom studies show why those decisions are shaped by evidence, time, people and the system around them.",
    hero: imageBlock(images.hero, "The school day is built from decisions too small to appear on most timetables."),
    blocks: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A school timetable is a useful fiction. It says mathematics begins at 9:10, English at 10:00, lunch at 12:30. It gives an institution a shape that can fit on one sheet of paper." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The actual day refuses the neatness of the grid. A student has misunderstood yesterday's idea. A parent message arrives just before class. The projector refuses to cooperate. A child who usually answers everything has gone quiet. The exercise that looked right on Tuesday is wrong for this group on Wednesday. Someone has five minutes between lessons and needs to decide what those five minutes are for." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Teaching is not only the delivery of planned work. It is the management of these deviations from the plan." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Scale makes the small decisions easy to miss" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "India's school system is large enough to make individual judgement disappear inside the numbers. UDISE+ recorded about 1.01 crore teachers, 14.71 lakh schools and 24.69 crore students in 2024–25. At that scale, a 'teacher decision' sounds almost too small to matter." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Yet the system only becomes real through those decisions. Someone decides whether a student needs another example. Someone decides that a question deserves another three minutes. Someone notices that a group has finished the task but has not understood it. Someone chooses not to interrupt a productive struggle." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The interesting question is not whether teachers make hundreds of decisions. They obviously do. It is what information those decisions are allowed to use." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "A classroom is an information problem" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Teachers rarely have complete information. They have partial signals: an answer, a pause, a raised hand, a notebook, a facial expression, a pattern repeated across three students. Good judgement is partly the ability to decide how much weight to give each signal." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That method resembles the work teachers do informally. The classroom is not a still photograph. It is a sequence. What was true at 9:15 may not be true at 9:23." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The decision can change when the evidence changes" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Consider a more formal example. At Salma Public School in Baghpat, Uttar Pradesh, ASER Centre assessed almost all 371 students in Grades 1 to 5 in April 2024, working with ten teachers. The assessment identified gaps in foundational learning. The response was not simply to ask teachers to work harder. Teachers in Grades 1 and 2 were trained in play-based pedagogy, while teachers in Grades 3 to 5 were trained in Teaching at the Right Level for remedial instruction. An endline assessment followed in April 2025." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The value of the example is not that one school's intervention can be copied everywhere. It is that the decision was tied to information about the students in front of the teachers. Evidence changed the description of the problem; the description changed the intervention." },
      { type: "quote", text: "The quality of a decision depends partly on the quality of the information a school makes available before the decision has to be made.", attribution: "Staffroom Review" },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Not every decision needs more data" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "There is a danger here. Once schools begin talking about evidence, it is tempting to measure everything. But more information is not automatically better information." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A teacher deciding whether to give a class another example may not need a dashboard. A colleague may simply notice that half the room has made the same error. A pastoral decision may depend on a conversation that cannot be reduced to a number. A school leader deciding whether a pattern is widespread may need something more systematic." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The useful distinction is between information and measurement. Measurement is one way of producing information. It is not the only one." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Time is part of judgement" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A decision can be perfectly sensible and still be impossible to make well if the person making it has no time to think." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This is one reason workload debates matter beyond teacher wellbeing. Fragmented time changes the quality of professional attention. If planning is repeatedly interrupted, teachers have fewer opportunities to examine what happened in the previous lesson and prepare a response. If every meeting ends with another task, the next decision begins with a smaller margin." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "UDISE+ can count teachers. A timetable can count periods. A calendar can count meetings. None of those measures tells us how much usable attention sits between them." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The decision not to change anything" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Some of the best decisions are refusals to react." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A student answers incorrectly but is thinking productively. A discussion wanders for two minutes and then reaches the question the teacher had hoped to ask. A lesson looks untidy because students are working independently rather than waiting for instructions." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Professional judgement includes knowing when a deviation is evidence of failure and when it is evidence that something useful is happening. That cannot be settled by appearance alone." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "And some decisions belong above the classroom" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "It is convenient to describe teaching quality as the sum of teacher choices. It is also incomplete." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A school decides how many non-teaching tasks sit inside the working week. It decides how information is collected. It decides which meetings are mandatory, how support is organised and how quickly new priorities are introduced. These choices determine the conditions in which classroom judgement is exercised." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The teacher at the front of the room is therefore both an individual decision-maker and the final link in a chain of institutional decisions." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Making judgement visible without turning it into a formula" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A useful professional conversation can stay close to the actual decision. What did you notice? What did you think it meant? What alternatives did you have? Why did you choose this response? What happened after you chose it?" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "These questions are more revealing than asking whether a lesson was 'good'. They also make room for uncertainty. A teacher can say, 'I thought this student was stuck, but I was not sure why.' That is a more honest account of professional work than a confident explanation invented after the fact." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The point of making decisions visible is not to turn teaching into a flowchart. It is to make expertise discussable." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "By the end of the day" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The decisions disappear quickly. The class moves on. The board is wiped. The next teacher arrives. The timetable keeps its clean lines." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "But the day has been shaped by choices that will never appear in the timetable: another example, another minute, a different seat, a question left open, a conversation postponed until the student was ready, a task made harder because the first version was too easy." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That is why the most useful way to think about teaching may be neither as performance nor as improvisation. It is judgement under conditions. Improve the information, protect the time, understand the people in the room—and the small decisions have a better chance of becoming good ones." },
    ],
    sources: [
      { label: "UDISE+ 2024–25, Ministry of Education, Government of India", url: "https://www.education.gov.in/sites/upload_files/mhrd/files/statistics-new/UDISE%2BReport%202024-25%20-%20NEP%20Structure.pdf" },
      { label: "ASER 2024, Annexure 11: Key findings from the classroom observations", url: "https://asercentre.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/Annexure_11.pdf" },
      { label: "ASER Centre: Baseline and endline assessment programme, Salma Public School, Baghpat", url: "https://asercentre.org/researches/baseline-and-endline-assessment-programme-salma-public-school-baghpat/" },
    ],
  },


  "SR-2026-0002": {
    readingTime: "8 min read",
    metaDescription: "Leaving school on time sounds like a small act until a teacher has spent years treating unfinished work as proof of care. The boundary is not simple, but it can be made visible.",
    hero: imageBlock(images.staffroom, "The working day does not always end when the timetable says it does."),
    blocks: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "For a long time, I measured the end of my school day by what was still on my desk. If there were books to mark, I stayed. If a parent message was waiting, I answered it. If tomorrow's lesson was not ready, I opened the laptop again. Leaving before everything was finished felt less like going home and more like walking away from something I was supposed to care about." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The trouble with that measure is that school work is almost infinitely expandable. There is always another exercise book. Another message. Another slide that could be improved. Another student whose situation deserves one more thought." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The job can make availability look like virtue" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Teaching contains a particular kind of moral pressure. The work is about other people, and the needs of those people rarely arrive in neat batches. A student remembers a question at 4:20. A colleague asks for help. A parent replies after dinner. A lesson that went badly follows you home because you can already see how you might have done it differently." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That does not mean every teacher is working unreasonable hours, or that every late evening is harmful. It means the boundary between professional care and permanent availability can become difficult to see." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The Indian evidence is less tidy than the slogan" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A study of 356 school teachers in Rajasthan, published in 2023, examined psychological well-being alongside energy management and stress. The researchers found that better energy management was positively associated with psychological well-being, with stress playing an important mediating role. The sample came from both rural and urban schools and included public and private teachers." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "It would be too much to turn that finding into a rule saying teachers should simply work less. The study was cross-sectional, and its participants were recruited through convenience sampling. But it does make one idea harder to dismiss: the way a teacher's energy is used is part of the conditions of the job, not merely a private matter of resilience." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "So I tried an experiment" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "I picked one evening and decided that I would leave when the school day ended. Not when the work ended. When the day ended." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The first surprise was how much unfinished work could survive without becoming a disaster. A few books stayed in a pile. One email waited until morning. Tomorrow's lesson was good enough rather than polished. Nothing dramatic happened." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The second surprise was that leaving on time did not make the work disappear. It changed its location. The unfinished task became a decision for tomorrow instead of a demand for tonight." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The boundary has to be designed" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A boundary is easier to keep when the work itself has a stopping rule. Marking can have a batch limit. Messages can have a response window. Planning can have a point at which the lesson is ready enough to teach. Some conversations can wait for the next school day." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "None of these rules is a substitute for judgement. A safeguarding concern does not become less urgent because the clock says four-thirty. A student in distress may need an adult who stays. The point is not to make every situation obey a boundary. It is to stop treating every unfinished task as if it were an emergency." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "What changed in the classroom" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The most useful change was not that I became more efficient. It was that I arrived with more attention. When the evening was no longer an extension of the school day by default, the next morning felt less like recovery from yesterday." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That matters because teaching is a job in which attention is part of the work. A teacher who is technically present but mentally carrying six unfinished tasks is still spending something." },
      { type: "quote", text: "Leaving on time did not teach me to care less. It taught me to stop using exhaustion as evidence that I cared.", attribution: "Staffroom Review" },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The work that can wait" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "I still stay late sometimes. The difference is that staying late is now a decision, not a moral default." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "There is a useful distinction between responsibility and availability. Responsibility means noticing what needs doing and making sure it is done. Availability means being reachable whenever something might need doing. Schools need the first. They cannot build a sustainable working life around the second." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The desk will never be empty. That may be the most freeing thing to learn. The aim is not to finish every possible piece of work. It is to finish the work that matters, leave the rest where it can be picked up again, and remember that a teacher is allowed to have a tomorrow that is not already exhausted by today." },
          { type: "paragraph", text: "Leaving on time did not happen because the work suddenly became smaller. It happened because I started noticing the difference between a task being unfinished and a task being urgent. Those are not the same category, although school days are very good at making them feel like one." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "There were papers that could be checked the next morning, messages that could be answered during the working day and planning decisions that became clearer after I had slept. The discipline was not to abandon those tasks. It was to stop using the length of my evening as the measure of whether I had done enough." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This also changed how I thought about colleagues. When one teacher is always available, the school begins to route problems towards that person. The behaviour can look generous from the outside while quietly teaching everyone else that someone will absorb the overflow. Boundaries are partly individual, but they are also signals about what an organisation considers reasonable." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "I still have evenings when the school day follows me home. A difficult conversation can stay in my mind. A student can need more support than a timetable allows. There are weeks when the work expands. The difference is that those moments now have names. They are particular demands, not proof that I should be permanently available." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The more sustainable habit is modest: decide what must be finished, decide what can wait, and be willing to let tomorrow contain some of today's unfinished work. The classroom does not become less important when the teacher goes home. Sometimes the teacher is better able to return to it because they did." },

    ],
    sources: [
      { label: "Enhancing psychological well-being of school teachers in India: role of energy management, thriving, and stress — study of 356 teachers in Rajasthan", url: "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10623443/" },
    ],
  },

  "SR-2026-0064": {
    readingTime: "7 min read",
    metaDescription: "A class can stay in a teacher's memory because of one small moment: a question, a pause, a student who suddenly sees the work differently.",
    hero: imageBlock(images.hero, "The class a teacher remembers is not always the class that looked exceptional on paper."),
    blocks: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "I do not remember the class because it was the loudest, the highest-achieving or the most difficult. I remember it because one morning the lesson went quiet in a way I had not planned for." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "I had explained the task twice. The room was settled. Books were open. The students appeared to be doing what students are supposed to do when a teacher has finished giving instructions. Then one child looked up and asked a question that exposed a problem with the explanation I had just given." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The question was not clever. It was better than clever. It was exact." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The moment the plan stopped being useful" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "My first instinct was to answer quickly and move on. The timetable was already moving. The task was supposed to take twenty minutes. I had another activity planned after it." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Instead, I asked the student to say the question again. Then I asked whether anyone else had the same problem. Several hands went up." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That was the class I remember: not because everyone suddenly became engaged, but because the room told me that my assumption about what had been understood was wrong." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Teachers are always working with incomplete information" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A classroom can make a teacher feel as though every student is visible. In reality, most of what a teacher knows arrives through partial signals: an answer, a silence, a notebook, a question, a face that changes when an example appears." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That is one reason structured classroom observation has become useful in parts of India's education system. A UNESCO-documented programme using the World Bank's Teach tool has supported classroom observations and mentoring in Andhra Pradesh, Maharashtra, Nagaland and Haryana. The model uses repeated observations of classroom culture, instruction and socio-emotional learning, followed by individualised feedback rather than treating observation only as inspection." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The important idea is not the technology. It is the feedback loop. A single lesson can be misleading. A pattern across observations can give a teacher something more useful to work with." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The class I remember taught me to look twice" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "After that lesson, I started paying more attention to the difference between compliance and understanding. A student can copy perfectly and still be lost. A student can interrupt and still be thinking well. A quiet room can be attentive. It can also be confused." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This sounds obvious when written down. It is much harder in the middle of a lesson, when there are thirty or forty students and a clock that refuses to stop." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The question became part of the lesson" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "I did not turn the student's question into a classroom legend. We went back to the example. I asked two students to explain what they thought it meant. Someone disagreed. Another student found a counterexample. The original task changed shape." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That was the useful part. The lesson did not become better because I abandoned the plan. It became better because the plan stopped being more important than the information the room was giving me." },
      { type: "quote", text: "The lesson I remember is the one that taught me that a plan can be wrong without the lesson being a failure.", attribution: "Staffroom Review" },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Memory is not the same as evidence" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "I also learned to be careful with the stories teachers tell about memorable classes. Memory compresses. It turns a sequence into a moment and a complicated room into a clean lesson." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That is why observation matters. If we want to improve teaching, we need more than the stories teachers remember afterwards. We need ways of looking closely at what happened while preserving enough context to understand why it happened." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "What stayed with me" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Years later, I cannot remember every exercise from that week. I cannot reconstruct the exact wording of the explanation that failed. I remember the pause after the question and the feeling that the room had just become more intelligent than my plan." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That is why some classes stay with teachers. Not because they prove that we taught brilliantly, but because they reveal something about the work that a plan cannot tell us in advance." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The best thing I could do with that memory was not to preserve it. It was to let it change what I looked for in the next room." },
    ],
    sources: [
      { label: "UNESCO SDG 4 Knowledge Hub: Strengthening teaching quality at scale through classroom observation and contextualised teacher support in India", url: "https://www.unesco.org/sdg4education2030/en/knowledge-hub/strengthening-teaching-quality-scale-through-classroom-observation-and-contextualised-teacher" },
    ],
  },

  "SR-2026-0066": {
    readingTime: "8 min read",
    metaDescription: "After the last student leaves, a second school day begins: marking, planning, records, messages, preparation and the small work that makes tomorrow possible.",
    hero: imageBlock(images.staffroom, "The school day does not end when the classroom empties."),
    blocks: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The last child leaves and the room changes character. Chairs are pushed back. The noise moves down the corridor. A board still carries the final question from the lesson. For a few minutes, it looks as though the work is finished." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "It is not. The visible class has ended. The invisible preparation for the next one has begun." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The second shift is made of fragments" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "There is no single task called 'after school work'. There are books to look through, attendance to complete, a parent to call, tomorrow's materials to prepare, a worksheet to print, a colleague to answer, a student to check on, a record to update." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Each task is small enough to sound harmless. Together they create a second working day that is difficult to see because it is distributed across dozens of small actions." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "India has measured some of this work" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A World Bank study of time use in India's government schools found that non-academic activities—including compiling and providing data, maintaining records and supervising the mid-day meal—accounted for 20–25% of teachers' reported weekly working hours. The study also found that, on average, about one-fifth of observed classroom time involved organisational or unrelated activity rather than teaching and learning tasks." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The study is not a current national estimate of every teacher's workload, and its fieldwork is older. Its value here is different: it shows that the distinction between 'teaching time' and 'everything else' has long been visible in Indian school systems." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The everything-else category is where much of the professional infrastructure sits. Records have to exist. Information has to move. Materials have to be ready. Families have to be contacted. The work is necessary, even when it is not the part of teaching most people imagine." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The task that looks smallest can block tomorrow" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A teacher may postpone ten minutes of planning because a meeting ran over. Ten minutes becomes twenty. A book is left unmarked because another urgent message arrives. Tomorrow's lesson is then planned around what can be assembled quickly rather than what the class actually needs." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This is why workload is not only a question of quantity. Fragmentation changes the order in which work gets done. It can push preparation into the last available space, when the teacher has the least attention left." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Not all invisible work should disappear" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "There is a temptation to treat every non-classroom task as waste. That would be too simple. A teacher calling home after a difficult incident is doing teaching-related work. A teacher reviewing yesterday's exercise books is preparing the next decision. A teacher writing down what a colleague needs to know is protecting continuity." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The useful question is not whether a task happens outside the lesson. It is whether the task contributes to the work the school is asking teachers to do, and whether it is organised in a way that leaves enough time for the work that only a teacher can do." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The last child is not the end of the teacher's attention" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The after-school period is where tomorrow is assembled from today's evidence. A notebook says one student needs another explanation. A marked exercise shows a misconception. A message from home changes the context. A colleague mentions that a student struggled in another class." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The teacher has to turn those fragments into a plan. That is professional judgement, not administrative overflow." },
      { type: "quote", text: "The work after the bell is often tomorrow's lesson being built out of today's leftovers.", attribution: "Staffroom Review" },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "What a school can choose to protect" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Schools cannot remove every task that sits outside teaching. They can make the invisible workload more visible. They can ask which records are actually used, which meetings require teachers, which messages need same-day responses and which preparation tasks need uninterrupted time." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The aim is not to make teachers do nothing after class. It is to make the work after class legible enough to manage." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "When the last student leaves, the teacher is not simply staying behind. The teacher is carrying the classroom forward into tomorrow. The question for a school is whether it has designed enough time and support for that work to be done without becoming a permanent second shift." },
    ],
    sources: [
      { label: "World Bank / Government of India: India — Secondary Education in India: Time on Teaching and Learning", url: "https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/414231492604976838/pdf/114392-WP-P145364-PUBLIC-ACS.pdf" },
    ],
  },

  "SR-2026-0016": {
    readingTime: "7 min read",
    metaDescription: "The staffroom is more than a break between lessons. It can be a place where teachers exchange practical knowledge, test ideas and make difficult days more manageable.",
    hero: imageBlock(images.school, "Professional knowledge often moves through conversations that never enter the official record."),
    blocks: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A staffroom can look like a room designed for tea. The useful work happening inside it is less obvious." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Someone asks how another teacher handled the same reading difficulty. Someone leaves a worksheet on the table. A colleague explains why a particular child is struggling with a task. Two teachers compare what happened in their classes and discover that the same problem looks different in each room." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The knowledge between the lessons" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Much professional knowledge is difficult to package. It is specific to a class, a timetable, a language, a school culture or a particular moment. A handbook can tell a teacher what a strategy is. A colleague can sometimes explain what it looks like at 11:20 on a Wednesday when the class is not responding." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That difference matters. Teachers do not only need information. They need help interpreting information in context." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "India is building more formal versions of this idea" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A UNESCO-documented foundational learning programme in Bengaluru district used multi-level resource teams, monthly planning and teacher learning circles during 2024–25. The programme combined baseline assessment with ongoing mentoring and peer learning, and reported a 12.48% improvement in the number of students achieving targeted foundational literacy and numeracy competencies between baseline and endline." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The important part is not that a learning circle automatically produces improvement. The programme was a specific intervention in a specific district, with a particular structure and support system. The useful lesson is that teacher collaboration becomes more valuable when it is connected to actual classroom evidence and a recurring opportunity to adapt practice." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The informal version has its own strengths" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The best staffroom conversations are rarely mini-seminars. They are short, concrete and unfinished. 'What did you do when she refused to read?' 'Have you tried putting the example before the explanation?' 'This worked with my class, but only after I changed the first question.'"},
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That kind of conversation respects the fact that teachers already know things. It does not assume professional learning begins whenever an expert enters the room." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "But the staffroom can also reproduce the wrong things" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Shared culture is not automatically good culture. A group can normalise cynicism as easily as it can normalise generosity. A rumour can travel faster than a useful idea. A teacher who is struggling may hear only that everyone else is coping." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This is why collegiality needs structure as well as goodwill. The conversation should have somewhere to go. A shared problem can become a question to test. A successful practice can be tried in another class. A concern can be taken to a mentor rather than repeated as a complaint." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The staffroom is not a substitute for leadership" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Schools sometimes rely on informal networks to compensate for weak systems. That is unfair to the teachers doing the compensating. If one experienced teacher is always the person everyone asks, the school has discovered a resource but not necessarily built a system." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Formal mentoring and peer learning can help turn individual knowledge into something more durable. The Bengaluru programme is one example. Other Indian systems are experimenting with structured classroom observation, mentor cadres and repeated feedback rather than one-off training days." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "What the room can make possible" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A good staffroom does not make every teacher agree. It makes it easier to ask a useful question without having to pretend you already know the answer." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "It gives teachers somewhere to bring the awkward middle of professional work: the lesson that was nearly right, the student who does not fit the plan, the idea that worked once but not twice." },
      { type: "quote", text: "A staffroom becomes useful when professional knowledge can move through it without having to become a performance.", attribution: "Staffroom Review" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That is a modest ambition for a room. It may also be one of the most practical forms of professional infrastructure a school can build." },
    ],
    sources: [
      { label: "UNESCO SDG 4 Knowledge Hub: Building strong foundations in Karnataka, India — FLN Reach Programme", url: "https://www.unesco.org/sdg4education2030/en/knowledge-hub/building-strong-foundations-inclusive-approach-early-literacy-and-numeracy-karnataka-india" },
    ],
  },

  "SR-2026-0009": {
    readingTime: "9 min read",
    metaDescription: "The fifth year can change how a teacher sees the job: less about surviving each week, more about deciding what kind of professional life is sustainable.",
    hero: imageBlock(images.staffroom, "Experience changes what a teacher notices—and what they are willing to keep doing."),
    blocks: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Five years into teaching, the useful question is rarely whether the work has become easy. It is whether the teacher has become better at choosing where effort belongs." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Five years is not a magical threshold. Careers do not change on an anniversary, and teachers do not become experienced in the same way. But the third-to-fifth-year period has long been studied because early-career decisions about staying, moving schools or leaving the profession can shape the stability of a teaching workforce." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Experience is not the same as certainty" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A useful misconception to lose is that experience simply makes teaching easier. It often makes the work more legible instead. A new teacher may experience a difficult class as a collection of surprises. A more experienced teacher starts to see patterns: which routines are failing, which students are drifting before a disruption becomes visible, which meetings will produce a decision and which will only produce another meeting." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That knowledge has value because it creates choices. The experienced teacher can decide what to prepare carefully and what can remain deliberately unfinished. They can distinguish a lesson that went badly from a lesson that revealed something useful. They can say no to some kinds of work because they know what saying yes will cost later." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The fifth year is also a test of the school" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Research on early-career retention repeatedly points beyond individual resilience. A 2026 longitudinal study of four early-career teachers in a hardest-to-staff Australian secondary school found that the teachers valued commitment, care and responsiveness to students while becoming increasingly frustrated by a leadership culture they experienced as compliance-focused and disconnected from classroom realities. All four eventually left that school, although they remained in teaching." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The lesson is not that teachers in year five inevitably leave. It is that professional commitment is partly relational. Teachers need evidence that the organisation around them understands the work they are being asked to do. Mentoring, useful feedback, manageable workload and collegial trust can matter as much as another individual resilience workshop." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "What changes when survival is no longer the main goal" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The most interesting fifth-year shift may be smaller. A teacher starts asking why a routine exists before automatically preserving it. They stop treating every piece of feedback as a verdict. They become more willing to reuse a successful explanation and more willing to abandon one that keeps failing. The classroom becomes less like a performance that has to be perfected and more like a place where decisions can be tested." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That does not mean lowering standards. It can mean placing standards where they belong. A teacher can care deeply about whether students understand a concept without believing every lesson must look impressive. They can care about parents without answering every message immediately. They can care about colleagues without becoming the person who absorbs every problem." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "A profession needs room to become a profession" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "India's recent education-policy direction also matters here. The more general lesson is that professional growth is easier when evidence helps people improve rather than simply ranking them. The Australian study is the specific evidence used in this article; it should not be stretched into a claim about Indian retention." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The fifth-year teacher, then, is not necessarily the teacher who knows everything. It may be the teacher who has learned what needs to be known, what can be learned later and what should not be carried alone." },
      { type: "quote", text: "Experience becomes useful when it creates more choices, not just more routines.", attribution: "Staffroom Review" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A school that wants teachers to stay cannot manufacture a five-year identity with a retention programme alone. It has to make the years worth staying for: enough autonomy to exercise judgement, enough support to keep learning, and enough time for teaching to remain the centre of the job." },
    
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Five years can also change how a teacher interprets failure. A lesson that would once have been evidence of incompetence can become evidence that a particular explanation, grouping or sequence did not work. That distinction matters because teaching produces too much variation for every outcome to be a verdict on the person doing the teaching." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The useful habit is to ask what the result makes possible next. If students misunderstood a concept, the next move may be a different representation rather than more repetition. If a routine is consuming time, the answer may be to change the routine rather than become faster at it. If a parent conversation has become circular, the teacher may need a clearer boundary or a different question. Experience does not remove problems; it makes experimentation safer." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That can be difficult in schools that equate consistency with sameness. A teacher who has learned to adapt may look less predictable from the corridor. Yet professional judgement is not supposed to be a performance of identical behaviour. It is the ability to make defensible choices under changing conditions." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The 2026 longitudinal study used a very small sample, so it should not be turned into a general law about teacher retention. Its more interesting contribution is the tension it captures between commitment to students and frustration with organisational conditions. A teacher can remain deeply committed to teaching while deciding that a particular way of organising teaching is not sustainable." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Schools sometimes answer this tension with wellbeing initiatives that sit outside the structure of the work. Those may be useful, but they do not substitute for manageable expectations, useful professional feedback and time that allows teachers to exercise expertise. A teacher who is trusted to diagnose a learning problem but given no time to discuss it is being trusted in theory rather than in practice." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The fifth year can therefore be less about becoming an expert and more about becoming selective. Some tasks deserve depth. Some deserve delegation. Some deserve a boundary. Some deserve another attempt because the underlying purpose is important. That selectivity is one way a profession becomes sustainable." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The question after five years is not whether the teacher has finally figured teaching out. It is whether the teacher can tell the difference between work that should be carried forward and work that should be put down." },
    ],
    sources: [
      { label: "When ‘quality’ collides: why do committed early career teachers leave? (Taylor & Francis, 2026)", url: "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/14681366.2026.2639575" },
    ],
  },

  "SR-2026-0011": {
    readingTime: "10 min read",
    metaDescription: "Generative AI did not invent the problems of homework. It exposed a deeper question: what is an assignment supposed to make a student think through?",
    hero: imageBlock(images.visual, "The useful question is not only whether AI was used, but what the assignment was designed to make the learner do."),
    blocks: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A homework sheet has not changed its shape just because generative AI arrived. There may still be ten questions, a paragraph to analyse or an essay due on Monday. What has changed is the number of ways a student can arrive at something that looks finished." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That creates an integrity problem, but integrity is not the whole story. Homework was never valuable merely because a student could submit a correct answer. Its value depended on what happened in the student's head while getting there." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "AI changed the shortcut, not the underlying problem" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A 2025 study of 548 secondary students across 30 Hungarian classes found that 16% had used AI to complete text-based homework. The study is not an estimate for India, and it should not be treated as one. Its value is narrower: it provides empirical evidence that AI-assisted homework is already a measurable classroom behaviour rather than a hypothetical future problem." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The larger point is that students have always found ways to reduce the effort of assignments that feel repetitive, disconnected or impossible. Search engines changed copying. Answer sites changed how worked solutions circulated. Private tuition changed what could be completed outside school. Generative AI makes the substitution faster and more convincing." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The assignment may be asking the wrong question" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "If a student can paste a prompt into a chatbot and receive an acceptable answer without needing to understand the material, the machine has exposed something about the task. It may still have a legitimate purpose, but the task is not reliably measuring independent understanding." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This is why the most useful response is not simply to teach teachers how to detect AI prose. Detection turns the classroom into an arms race. A more durable response changes what counts as evidence of learning: a first attempt, a correction, an explanation in the student's own words, a comparison between two answers, a reflection on an error, or a short oral defence of a written solution." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "There is a place for AI in homework" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "UNESCO's guidance on generative AI recommends using these tools to reduce unnecessary pressure around homework and exams rather than increasing it, while also emphasising ethical validation, human accountability and AI literacy. That is a more useful position than either enthusiasm or prohibition." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A student might ask an AI system for three explanations of a difficult idea and then identify which explanation is most accurate. They might ask it to generate a deliberately flawed solution and annotate the errors. They might compare their own paragraph with an AI-generated version and explain which is clearer and why. In these cases, the machine supplies material; the student still has to judge it." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Homework can become evidence of thinking" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The strongest redesign is often simple. Ask for the working, not just the answer. Ask what changed between the first and final attempt. Bring one question from home back into class. Require a short reflection on the most difficult decision. Let the task be unfinished in a productive way." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "These moves also reduce the temptation to pretend that all homework is independent. A student working with a parent, sibling, tutor, textbook or AI tool is not necessarily learning less. The important question is what responsibility remains with the learner." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The old homework problem remains" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "There is another reason not to make AI the entire story. Homework can already be excessive, repetitive and unequal. Some students have quiet rooms, reliable internet and adult help. Others share devices, care for siblings, travel long distances or simply have nowhere comfortable to work." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Generative AI can widen those differences, but it can also reveal them. If the only way to make an assignment manageable is to outsource it, the problem is not solely the software. It is also the design and volume of the work." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The homework question after AI is therefore less dramatic than 'How do we stop students cheating?' It is more demanding: what do we want students to practise when nobody is standing beside them? If the answer is judgement, explanation, revision and curiosity, the assignment has to make those things visible." },
    
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Homework also carries a hidden assumption: that the finished product is a trustworthy trace of the learner's thinking. That assumption was already imperfect before generative AI. A student can reproduce an answer from a website, copy a model paragraph, ask another person for help or spend an hour following an example without understanding why it works." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The new technology makes that gap more visible because the final product can arrive so quickly. It is tempting to respond by making assignments longer, more complicated or more handwritten. But adding friction is not the same as adding learning. A poorly designed task can remain poorly designed even when it takes twice as long to complete." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A better design starts with the cognitive work. If the aim is retrieval, ask for retrieval. If the aim is explanation, make the explanation observable. If the aim is revision, collect the first and second versions. If the aim is judgement, ask the student to compare alternatives and defend a decision. The task then has a clearer relationship to the learning it is meant to elicit." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "There is also a difference between using AI as a source of language and using it as a substitute for judgement. A student may ask for a counterargument, then decide whether it is fair. A student may ask for a worked example, then locate the step that fails. A language learner may compare an AI translation with a teacher's version and explain the difference. These are not automatically good activities, but they keep the learner's evaluation in the centre." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "UNESCO's guidance is useful here because it treats generative AI as an educational technology that requires human accountability and AI literacy rather than as a magic solution or an automatic threat. For schools, that means policies should be understandable enough that students know what is permitted and why, while assignments should still be designed so that permitted assistance does not erase the learning objective." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The fairness question matters too. A student who has quiet space, broadband, an adult tutor and multiple devices has a different homework environment from a student sharing one phone with siblings. The technology may magnify that difference. So can a rule that assumes every student can produce the same amount of independent work in the same conditions." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That is why homework reform should not begin with a detector. It should begin with the sentence: this task is asking students to practise ____. The blank forces a teacher to name the learning. Once that is clear, it becomes easier to decide what assistance is legitimate and what would simply bypass the point." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The result may be smaller homework, more purposeful homework or sometimes no homework at all. The point is not to defend the assignment as a tradition. It is to make the learning worth the student's time." },
    ],
    sources: [
      { label: "Turós, Nagy & Szűts — What percentage of secondary school students do their homework with the help of artificial intelligence? (Computers and Education: Artificial Intelligence, 2025)", url: "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666920X25000347" },
      { label: "UNESCO — Guidance for generative AI in education and research", url: "https://unesdoc.unesco.org/in/rest/annotationSVC/DownloadWatermarkedAttachment/attach_import_d4cbd94e-e183-448f-90a9-ea9bb3b74db2?_=386693eng&from=1&to=48" },
    ],
  },

  "SR-2026-0043": {
    readingTime: "9 min read",
    metaDescription: "When mathematics time is tight, teachers make choices about what to explain deeply, what to practise and what to leave for later. Those choices are part of teaching the subject.",
    hero: imageBlock(images.maths, "Teaching less can mean choosing more carefully what deserves time."),
    blocks: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A mathematics lesson can be perfectly on schedule and still leave a class one idea short." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "So a teacher makes a decision that is rarely described as curriculum work: what not to spend time on." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Coverage and understanding are not the same thing" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The pressure to finish a syllabus is understandable. Students need access to the full curriculum, examinations create deadlines and schools need some common expectations. But mathematical understanding does not always move at the speed of a scheme of work." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A 2019 study of mathematics classrooms in four CBSE secondary schools in Bhubaneswar found that teachers considered the prescribed Class IX content appropriate and well sequenced, yet classroom observations also showed limitations in activity-based learning and a ritualistic approach to lesson planning. Geometry was reported as particularly difficult to transact for teachers and students." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That does not prove that teachers are teaching too much mathematics. It shows something more useful: a curriculum can be coherent on paper while the work of making it learnable remains difficult." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "What gets protected when time gets tight" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "When a class is behind, the easiest response is often acceleration. Explain faster. Assign more. Move the examples to homework. Reduce discussion. Skip the question that opens an unexpected line of reasoning." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The problem is that mathematics accumulates. A student who has memorised a procedure without understanding why it works may appear ready for the next chapter. The gap only becomes visible when the new idea depends on the old one." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "NCERT's position on mathematics has long emphasised mathematisation, meaningful problems, communication and reasoning rather than reducing school mathematics to formulas and mechanical procedures. Recent NCERT research likewise stresses mathematical thinking and the ability to apply mathematics in unfamiliar situations." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Less can mean deeper" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Teaching less mathematics does not have to mean lowering ambition. It can mean choosing a smaller number of ideas and allowing students to work on them from several directions: represent the problem, explain a method, compare two methods, find an error, invent a counterexample, apply the idea somewhere unfamiliar." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That kind of lesson may produce fewer completed exercises. It can also produce more information about what students actually understand." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The teacher needs permission to choose" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Curriculum flexibility is not the same as curriculum neglect. A teacher who chooses to slow down should be able to explain why: this concept is foundational; the class has a misconception; the assessment evidence shows a gap; the next unit depends on this one." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The reverse is also true. Sometimes moving quickly is the right decision. A teacher may know that a routine skill is secure and that the class will learn more from using it in a new context than from another page of practice." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This is where experience and subject knowledge meet. Good pacing is not a fixed number of minutes per topic. It is the ability to judge what the learners in front of you need next." },
      { type: "quote", text: "The real question is not how much mathematics a teacher can fit into a term, but how much mathematics a learner can carry forward.", attribution: "Staffroom Review" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "If schools want mathematical understanding rather than merely mathematical coverage, they have to protect the professional judgement that makes selective depth possible. Sometimes teaching less is not a retreat from the curriculum. It is a more serious attempt to teach it." },
    
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The danger of curriculum pressure is not only that teachers rush. It is that rushing can become invisible. A lesson still begins. The pages still turn. Students still produce answers. The calendar still says the class is on schedule. The missing piece is the evidence that the idea has become usable knowledge." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A teacher making a pacing decision needs something more concrete than instinct. One useful source of evidence is the explanation students give when the teacher removes the scaffolding. Can they choose a method without being prompted? Can they explain why it works? Can they spot an error in a new problem? Can they represent the same idea in another form?" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This makes assessment part of pacing rather than a separate event. A quick check can reveal that the class is ready to move. Another can reveal that apparent fluency is only recognition. The teacher can then protect time for the concept that will otherwise become a bottleneck later." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The NCERT research on mathematics classroom processes is valuable partly because it looked beyond curriculum documents into actual classroom practice. The curriculum was not simply declared too hard or too large. Instead, the researchers found a more complicated picture in which content, pedagogy, classroom conditions and teacher practice interact." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That is a useful model for the question in this story. 'Teach less mathematics' should never mean simply remove chapters. It means decide where depth has the highest return, where practice needs to be distributed and where a routine is already secure. The decision should be explainable from the learning evidence." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "There is also a professional cost to permanent acceleration. Teachers begin to plan for what can be covered rather than what can be understood. Students learn to look for the procedure that matches the chapter heading. The subject starts to feel like a sequence of gates rather than a connected way of thinking." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A more deliberate pace can be especially valuable in geometry and other areas where representation, reasoning and language matter. The recent NCERT discussion of geometry learning outcomes emphasises the relationship between curricular expectations, evidence of student understanding and pedagogical choices. That is a better description of the teacher's job than simply counting completed exercises." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The practical choice is therefore not between coverage and caring less. It is between treating every minute as interchangeable and using professional judgement to decide what the next minute should buy." },
    ],
    sources: [
      { label: "Krishnan & Kapoor — How do Children Learn Mathematics? (Journal of Indian Education, 2019)", url: "https://ejournals.ncert.gov.in/index.php/jie/article/view/2656" },
      { label: "NCERT — Learning Outcomes in Geometry at Secondary Stage", url: "https://ejournals.ncert.gov.in/index.php/ET/article/view/3657" },
    ],
  },

  "SR-2026-0006": {
    readingTime: "10 min read",
    metaDescription: "Repeatedly sending a child out of class can remove the immediate disruption while leaving the reasons for the behaviour—and the child's sense of belonging—unexamined.",
    hero: imageBlock(images.classroom, "Removal can solve the noise in a room without solving the problem that produced it."),
    blocks: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The door closes. The room becomes quieter. The lesson continues. For the child outside, the lesson has changed completely." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Sending a student out can be necessary in a difficult moment. Safety matters. Other students deserve to learn. Teachers need ways to interrupt behaviour that is escalating. The harder question begins when removal becomes the routine response to the same child." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "A quiet classroom can hide a recurring problem" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The immediate benefit of removal is visible. The child is no longer interrupting the lesson. What is less visible is the learning time lost by the child, the relationship that may become harder to repair and the possibility that the behaviour is communicating a difficulty the school has not yet understood." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Research on behaviour is strongest at showing patterns and intervention effects, not diagnosing an individual student. That is why the useful response is to combine a clear boundary with disciplined observation of what happens before and after the incident." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Those findings concern broader systems and cannot be used to explain one particular child. They do, however, change the question a teacher or school leader might ask: not only 'How do we stop this behaviour?' but 'What happens to this child when this becomes the way we respond?'" },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The label arrives quickly" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Once a child becomes known as difficult, the label can start doing work. Adults anticipate trouble. The child's ordinary behaviour is interpreted through the previous incident. A request can sound defiant. A joke can sound disruptive. A mistake can become evidence that the child is not trying." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This does not mean that behaviour has no consequences. It means that consequences work better when the school can distinguish the behaviour from the person. 'He disrupted the lesson' is an observation. 'He is disruptive' is an identity claim." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "What the evidence cannot tell a school" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Research on exclusion is strongest at showing patterns and associations, not diagnosing an individual student. A systematic review of school-based interventions found that mentoring, monitoring and teacher skills training showed promising short-term reductions in exclusion, while longer-term effects were less certain. That is a reason for caution, not a ready-made programme." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The practical implication is to widen the school's response. Before deciding that a child needs more punishment, examine when the behaviour happens, what tasks precede it, which adults can establish a productive relationship, what the child can already do successfully and whether the classroom environment is making a particular difficulty harder." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Belonging is not softness" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A school can hold a firm boundary and still communicate that the child belongs there. The two are not opposites. A student can be removed from a situation for safety and then returned to the learning community through a deliberate repair process." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That repair matters because exclusion can become self-reinforcing. The more a child misses instruction, the harder the next task may feel. The harder the task feels, the more likely disengagement becomes. The school then reads the disengagement as further evidence that removal was justified." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The better question is not whether a child ever leaves the room. It is whether the school's response helps the child return with a clearer route back into learning." },
      { type: "quote", text: "A behaviour label becomes dangerous when it explains the child more easily than the child's behaviour explains the situation.", attribution: "Staffroom Review" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The child who is always being sent out of class may indeed need firmer boundaries. They may also need a different question. Schools should be willing to investigate both." },
    
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The hardest part of a repeated removal pattern is that it can feel efficient. The adult gets a calmer room. The student leaves. The lesson continues. Everyone can tell what happened. Efficiency can hide the fact that the same problem has simply moved to another location." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Schools need a response that distinguishes the immediate safety decision from the longer learning question. A child may need to leave a room because behaviour has become unsafe. That does not settle what should happen tomorrow. The school still needs to examine the conditions around the incident and the route back to learning." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The Education Endowment Foundation's evidence on behaviour interventions points to the importance of approaches that are consistently implemented and supported through professional learning. That does not produce one recipe for an Indian school. It does suggest that behaviour response works better when adults share a coherent approach than when each incident is handled as a fresh personal judgement." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "UNICEF India's description of the quality challenge in schooling also matters because it places behaviour inside the wider question of whether classrooms are child-friendly and inclusive. A behaviour policy cannot be separated completely from the way students experience instruction, relationships, discipline and dignity." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The school should therefore collect small amounts of useful information. When does the behaviour happen? What task comes before it? Which adults respond? What happens after the student returns? Does the same pattern appear in mathematics, language, physical education and unstructured time? A week of disciplined observation can tell a school more than a year of reputation." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The aim is not to explain away behaviour. It is to make the next response more accurate. A firm boundary is easier to defend when adults can also explain what the boundary is protecting and what they will do to help the student succeed within it." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Belonging is not a reward for perfect behaviour. It is part of the condition under which behaviour can change. A student who knows there is a path back to the room has a different incentive from a student who has learned that one incident can become a permanent identity." },
    ],
    sources: [
      { label: "Education Endowment Foundation — Behaviour interventions", url: "https://educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk/education-evidence/teaching-learning-toolkit/behaviour-interventions" },
      { label: "UNICEF India — Quality education", url: "https://www.unicef.org/india/what-we-do/quality-education" },
    ],
  },

  "SR-2026-0013": {
    readingTime: "9 min read",
    metaDescription: "Calling a child difficult can make an adult's uncertainty feel settled. Changing the question can make the child's behaviour easier to understand and respond to.",
    hero: imageBlock(images.school, "The useful shift is from judging a child to examining what the child is communicating through behaviour."),
    blocks: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A label can close a case before the evidence is finished. In schools, two words such as 'difficult child' can quietly become a prediction about what will happen next." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The label can feel useful because it compresses a complicated pattern into two words. Difficult child. Attention seeker. Lazy. Defiant. But the convenience of the label is also its danger: once the explanation feels complete, adults may stop collecting information." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Describe the behaviour before explaining it" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A child who leaves a seat repeatedly is doing something observable. A child who refuses written work is doing something observable. 'He doesn't care' is not an observation; it is a conclusion." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The distinction matters because the same behaviour can have different causes. A refusal may follow a task the child cannot yet do. A joke may be an attempt to recover status after an embarrassing mistake. A child who appears inattentive may be managing an environment that has become overwhelming." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "None of these possibilities should be assumed. They should be tested." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "What happens before the behaviour?" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A useful school-level investigation is often almost boring: record what happened immediately before, what the student did, how adults responded and what happened next. Patterns emerge when the same question is asked repeatedly." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The evidence is also a warning against assuming that a written behaviour policy automatically determines what students experience. What matters is the climate produced by the ordinary practices that follow the policy." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The child is not the only variable" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Classroom behaviour is produced in a relationship between a learner and an environment. That environment includes the task, the teacher's instructions, peer dynamics, the physical room, the timetable, transitions and the consequences attached to different actions." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This does not remove responsibility from the student. It makes responsibility more precise. A school can hold a student accountable for hitting another child while also asking why the pattern keeps occurring and what adults need to change." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Labels can become self-fulfilling" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "When adults expect a student to fail, they may shorten explanations, offer fewer chances to answer or intervene earlier. The student then receives less successful learning experience and more correction. The cycle can make the original label look increasingly accurate." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The answer is not to pretend every child is misunderstood. It is to make the evidence richer than the label. What can the student do? When does the problem not occur? Which adult relationships work? What happens when the task changes? What does the child say about the moment, if given a safe way to explain it?" },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "A better question is still a demanding one" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Replacing 'difficult child' with 'what is happening here?' is not a softer standard. It can be harder because it requires adults to remain curious after they have become tired." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The most useful schools are not those with no difficult behaviour. They are schools where a difficult pattern can remain a question long enough for adults to learn something from it." },
      { type: "quote", text: "A label closes the case. A good question keeps the evidence coming.", attribution: "Staffroom Review" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The child who stopped being called difficult is not necessarily a different child. The adults may simply have become more precise about what they were seeing." },
    
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The most useful replacement for a label is not a kinder label. It is a better description of what actually happened. That sounds administrative, but it changes the quality of the conversation. A team can act on a pattern. It is much harder to act on a personality judgement." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Suppose a teacher reports that a student 'always refuses'. The next question should be when. Does refusal occur at the beginning of independent writing? During transitions? When the task changes without warning? Only in front of particular peers? Once the timing changes from a general character statement to a specific pattern, new options appear." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The same principle applies to adult response. If a teacher consistently sends the student out, the record may show that removal is happening before other strategies are attempted. If another teacher sees fewer incidents, the difference becomes something to investigate rather than proof that one person is simply better at behaviour." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "EEF research into school behaviour policies is relevant because it distinguishes written policy from the climate produced by everyday practice. A school can have a generous policy and still create a punitive experience if the ordinary response to difficulty is public correction, removal and repeated reminders of past incidents." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That is why behaviour review should include the student's experience without requiring the school to accept every explanation the student gives. What did the student think was happening? What did they understand the task to be? What do they believe the teacher expected? Where does their account align with observation, and where does it not? These questions add evidence." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The purpose of curiosity is not endless delay. Schools still have timetables, safety obligations and other students. The point is to stop treating certainty as a prerequisite for action. A school can act firmly while keeping the explanation provisional." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A label becomes especially powerful when it travels. It moves from one teacher to another, from a classroom note to a meeting, from a meeting to a parent conversation. Each repetition makes the original interpretation feel more like fact. A better system keeps returning to observable behaviour and current evidence." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The adult's question can therefore change from 'Why is this child like this?' to 'What keeps happening, under what conditions, and what response changes the pattern?' That question is harder. It is also more useful." },
    ],
    sources: [
      { label: "Education Endowment Foundation — Understanding how school policies and practices shape school behaviour", url: "https://educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk/projects-and-evaluation/projects/understanding-the-use-of-school-behavioural-policies-that-aim-to-enable-an-authoritative-school-climate" },
    ],
  },

  "SR-2026-0008": {
    readingTime: "10 min read",
    metaDescription: "Schools need numbers. They also need leaders who know when a measurement is illuminating the work and when it is merely creating another thing to report.",
    hero: imageBlock(images.schoolFeature, "The point of measurement is not to make a school look controlled. It is to help people decide what to do next."),
    blocks: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A school can produce a surprising amount of evidence without becoming much better at learning from it." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The problem is not measurement itself. A school without evidence is forced to rely on memory, anecdote and whoever speaks most confidently in the room. The problem begins when collecting a number becomes easier to defend than deciding what the number is for." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Not every number answers the same question" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "An attendance rate can tell a leader how often students were present. It cannot, by itself, tell them why a student was absent or whether a particular intervention will work. A test score can show performance on a defined assessment. It cannot become a complete description of a learner." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "School leaders therefore need to distinguish useful evidence from compliance evidence. A measure can be technically accurate and still be a poor guide to action if nobody knows what decision it is meant to inform." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The principal's job is partly deciding what not to measure" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A leader who removes a report is not necessarily becoming less accountable. They may be protecting attention for a better question. If a weekly spreadsheet is never discussed in a meeting, never changes a decision and cannot be traced to an action, it deserves scrutiny." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A measure becomes valuable when it changes what a school does. That means the principal has to define the decision first and then ask which evidence is actually necessary." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Measurement can still be uncomfortable" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Good data sometimes makes a school change its mind. A programme that everyone likes may not be helping the students it was intended to help. A class may be completing work but misunderstanding the concept. A subgroup may be improving while the overall average looks unchanged." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "That is why leaders need to distinguish signal from noise. They need enough context to interpret a result and enough humility to say when the evidence is insufficient." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "A school needs a small set of live questions" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "One practical discipline is to begin with decisions rather than dashboards. What are we trying to improve? What would tell us whether the change is helping? How often will we look? Who will act on the information? What would make us stop the intervention?" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This makes measurement smaller and more consequential. It also changes the relationship between leaders and teachers. Data becomes something used with professionals rather than something collected about them." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The wider the set of responsibilities, the more important it is to protect attention for the evidence that can actually change a decision." },
      { type: "quote", text: "The most mature measurement system is not the one with the most numbers. It is the one that changes a decision for a reason people can explain.", attribution: "Staffroom Review" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The principal who stops measuring everything has not stopped caring about evidence. The opposite may be true. They have decided that numbers deserve a purpose before they deserve a place in the school." },
    
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The problem with a school full of measurements is not that numbers are cold. It is that numbers can become comforting. A filled-in column looks like progress even when nobody can say what decision the column changed." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The 2026 OECD work on school improvement offers a useful counterpoint: high-quality data matters when it reaches schools, is interpreted collectively and is supported by professional development and autonomy. The important verb is interpreted. Data arrives with context missing, definitions to understand and questions to ask." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A principal looking at attendance may need to ask whether the headline rate hides a concentration of absence among one year group. A behaviour measure may need to be separated by location, time and type of incident. A reading assessment may need to be examined for the particular skills that produced the result. The useful question is not 'What is the number?' but 'What decision would this number justify?'" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This is where measurement becomes a leadership practice rather than an administrative habit. Leaders decide what deserves to be monitored closely, what can be sampled and what can be stopped. They also decide which measures should remain private working evidence and which should become shared evidence for teachers, students or families." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A smaller measurement set can also make professional conversation better. A department meeting can spend fifteen minutes discussing one meaningful pattern rather than forty minutes reviewing a dashboard that nobody owns. Teachers can disagree with an interpretation and offer contextual information. The data becomes something the school reasons with rather than something it reports upward." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Stopping a measure can feel risky because absence is often mistaken for lack of accountability. But accountability is stronger when a school can explain what it is accountable for. A school that collects everything may have difficulty showing what it actually learned." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The principal who stops measuring everything is therefore not anti-data. They are making the opposite claim: evidence deserves attention only when it can improve a decision. That requires leaders to tolerate smaller dashboards, fewer reports and more questions about meaning." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A good measurement culture leaves room for uncertainty. Sometimes the evidence is too small. Sometimes the indicator is noisy. Sometimes two signals point in different directions. A mature school can say so without immediately inventing a third metric to make the discomfort disappear." },
    ],
    sources: [
      { label: "OECD — Empowering schools’ improvement: Insights from PISA for Schools case studies (2026)", url: "https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/empowering-schools-improvement_50f6b0e5-en.html" },
    ],
  },

  "SR-2026-0075": {
    readingTime: "9 min read",
    metaDescription: "Parents often ask for marks because marks are legible. The harder task for schools is explaining the learning, uncertainty and next steps behind them.",
    hero: imageBlock(images.storyC, "A number travels easily between school and home. Learning is harder to compress."),
    blocks: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Two report cards can carry the same 82 and tell very different stories." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "What the number cannot easily carry is the story underneath it: what the student understands, what they are still learning, how confidently they can apply an idea, where the error came from and what should happen next." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Marks are useful precisely because they are simple" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The simplicity is not a flaw. Families need ways to understand progress. Teachers need assessment information. Schools need to communicate. The problem begins when a convenient summary becomes the only language available." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A mark can be useful for reporting while still being incomplete for teaching. The school should be explicit about that difference rather than expecting one number to do both jobs." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "India's assessment reform is trying to widen the conversation" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The National Curriculum Framework for School Education describes a move from assessment centred on rote memorisation towards regular, formative and competency-based assessment. It also describes a holistic progress card that can combine teacher assessment with self-assessment, peer assessment, projects, portfolios and other evidence." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "CBSE's SAFAL framework similarly describes assessment as diagnostic information that schools can use to identify learning gaps, adjust teaching and target support. The point is not to abolish marks. It is to stop asking marks to answer questions they cannot answer alone." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Parents still have a legitimate question" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A parent who asks 'How many marks did my child get?' is not necessarily asking for a ranking. Often they are asking a more basic question: Is my child doing okay? Do I need to help? Is something being missed? What should we do now?" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Schools can respond to that question without pretending that every form of progress can be reduced to a number. A useful parent conversation might pair the score with one demonstrated strength, one learning gap and one specific next step. That makes the assessment actionable." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The danger is comparison without context" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Two students with the same mark may need completely different support. A student who moved from 42 to 62 has a different story from one who moved from 82 to 62. A child who scores 90 on routine questions may need a more challenging opportunity than a child who scores 90 after mastering a new concept with substantial support." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The same applies across classes and schools. A number only becomes meaningful when the assessment, the learning goal and the context are understood." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "A better parent meeting does not hide the mark" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "It gives the mark somewhere to go. What does this result tell us? What does it not tell us? What evidence would we want next? What can the teacher change? What can the student practise? What should the family avoid turning into pressure?" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This is slower than reading a rank list. It is also closer to the purpose of assessment as described in India's current reform framework: evidence that helps children learn rather than a number that becomes the child." },
      { type: "quote", text: "A mark is a useful signal. It becomes a poor language when it is asked to describe the whole child.", attribution: "Staffroom Review" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Parents will keep asking for marks because marks are clear. The responsibility of the school is not to make that question disappear. It is to make the conversation larger." },
    
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A parent may ask for marks because marks are the part of school that is easiest to carry home. They can be remembered, compared and repeated. The problem is not the desire for clarity. It is what happens when the clarity of the number is mistaken for the completeness of the explanation." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "PARAKH's current work on holistic progress cards gives schools another way to think about this. Progress can include observations of abilities, self-assessment, peer assessment and evidence from different kinds of classroom activity. The purpose is not to make assessment vague. It is to make it more representative of the learning that actually happened." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The distinction becomes especially important when two students have the same score. One may have arrived at the answer through a secure method. Another may have guessed correctly and be one misconception away from failure on a harder task. The mark is identical. The instructional response should not automatically be." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This also changes the parent-teacher meeting. Instead of reading a sequence of scores, the conversation can connect evidence to next steps: what is going well, what remains fragile, what the student can do independently, and what support will help without turning every difficulty into pressure." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A wider assessment conversation does not mean withholding marks. It means placing them where they are useful. A school can still communicate a result clearly while explaining what the result does and does not establish." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The PARAKH example of learners demonstrating different kinds of understanding is useful because it shows why process matters. A correct answer is one form of evidence. Recognising and correcting an error is another. Explaining why two solutions differ is another. A school that records only the final answer throws away information it already has." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The family deserves that richer information because the purpose of the report is not simply to produce a ranking. It is to help adults decide what to do next. The number starts the conversation. It should not end it." },
    ],
    sources: [
      { label: "PARAKH — When the Progress Card Reads the Classroom: AI in the Preparatory Stage (2026)", url: "https://parakh.ncert.gov.in/blog/when-progress-card-reads-classroom-ai-preparatory-stage" },
    ],
  },

  "SR-2026-0005": {
    readingTime: "8 min read",
    metaDescription: "A school timetable is not just a scheduling document. It determines which subjects, activities, people and kinds of learning receive protected time.",
    hero: imageBlock(images.storyD, "Every timetable is a set of choices about what a school can afford to give time to."),
    blocks: [
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Give a timetable a few weeks and its priorities become visible without anyone announcing them." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "But a timetable is one of the most concrete statements a school makes about what matters. It decides not only what students study, but when they study it, how often they encounter it, whether teachers have time to prepare together and what kinds of activities can survive a crowded week." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Time is a curriculum decision" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "If a school gives a subject three periods a week, that is not merely an administrative fact. It is a curriculum allocation. If collaborative planning repeatedly loses its slot to urgent meetings, the school has made another choice, even if nobody wrote it down." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The OECD's work on curriculum and instruction time makes the broader point directly: decisions about instructional time reflect priorities about what students should learn and when. At school level, those choices become more granular." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "The timetable can reveal priorities more honestly than a mission statement" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Schools often say that creativity, physical activity, discussion and teacher collaboration matter. The timetable reveals whether those commitments have protected time. A subject can be valued in principle while receiving the kind of slot that makes it almost impossible to teach well." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The reverse can also happen. A school can quietly protect a practice that its formal documents barely mention because leaders know that it matters to the day-to-day experience of students and teachers." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "Rigid does not mean organised" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A timetable has to coordinate teachers, rooms, students and time. Some rigidity is necessary. But rigidity can become a substitute for thinking. When every lesson is forced into the same length, the schedule can start dictating the pedagogy rather than supporting it." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Some learning benefits from short, repeated practice. Some requires a longer uninterrupted period. A science investigation, a piece of art or a field-based project may not fit neatly into a single standard period. A timetable that cannot accommodate variation may quietly privilege the kinds of learning that fit the timetable most easily." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "What teachers see that the grid does not" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Teachers know where the timetable hurts. The class that always arrives after a long transition. The subject repeatedly placed when attention is low. The planning period consumed by cover. The room change that takes ten minutes. The double period that looks generous until equipment has to be moved." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "These are not minor logistical annoyances. They determine how much of the nominal teaching time becomes actual learning time." },
      { type: "heading", level: 2, text: "A timetable should be revisable" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The best timetable is not necessarily the one that survives the year unchanged. Schools learn. Cohorts change. staffing changes. A new intervention may need protected time. A subject may discover that a different rhythm produces better work." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Revising a timetable is difficult because every change affects several people at once. That is precisely why schools should treat the timetable as a live expression of priorities rather than a sacred document produced once a year." },
      { type: "quote", text: "The timetable is a map of values because every protected hour tells someone what the school is willing to protect.", attribution: "Staffroom Review" },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A school cannot give everything unlimited time. The point is not to pretend otherwise. It is to make the trade-offs visible enough that the timetable becomes a deliberate educational choice rather than an accident of the calendar." },
    
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A timetable becomes revealing when you stop reading it as a schedule and start reading it as a sequence of trade-offs. Every protected period has an opportunity cost. Time given to one activity cannot simultaneously be given to another." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The OECD notes that countries make choices about how much time is devoted to instruction and that those choices reflect priorities about what students should learn and when. At school level, those choices become more granular: which subject gets a long block, when collaborative planning is possible, how transitions are handled and which activities are treated as expendable." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A school can say it values interdisciplinary work while scheduling every subject into isolated fragments. It can say teacher collaboration matters while using the same slot for emergency cover. It can say students need recovery time while stacking demanding lessons without a pause. None of these contradictions necessarily result from bad intentions. They can arise from a timetable that has never been revisited after circumstances changed." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The issue is not simply flexibility versus rigidity. A timetable needs enough structure to coordinate people and rooms. But structure should support educational purpose rather than become the purpose itself." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "Teachers often see the hidden cost first. Ten minutes lost in a room change is rarely recorded as a curricular decision, but ten minutes removed from every lesson in a week adds up. A practical subject squeezed into the last available slot may have to spend its opening minutes recovering attention rather than teaching." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "This is why timetable review should include teachers and not only administrators. The grid shows allocation. Teachers can describe what the allocation actually produces. A period that looks generous on paper may disappear into setup; a short period may work beautifully because the routine is stable." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "A revisable timetable is therefore a form of school learning. It allows leaders to ask whether the current arrangement still serves the current students, curriculum and staff. The answer may be no even when the timetable was once perfectly sensible." },
      { type: "paragraph", text: "The grid is never only a grid. It is a record of the educational choices a school has decided to make visible through time." },
    ],
    sources: [
      { label: "OECD — Curriculum and instruction time", url: "https://www.oecd.org/en/topics/curriculum-and-instruction-time.html" },
    ],
  },
};

export function getStoryArticle(storyId) {
  return storyArticles[storyId] || null;
}
